2026-09-20 syncfs design correction: neither unconditional forwarding of unmarked synthetic regular-proc memfds nor a blanket TMPFS_MAGIC success shortcut proves full Linux parity. Linux syncfs reports and advances sb->s_wb_err; mapping_set_error records mapping errors there, and dirty shmem page hardware-poison handling can set EIO. Therefore an ordinary memfd opened before that error must receive EIO once and advance its per-file cursor, while a synthetic proc descriptor must retain procfs no-op success. Blanket tmpfs success hides the ordinary error/cursor transition; forwarding an unmarked synthetic proc memfd can leak unrelated shmem EIO. Historical https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/534 overgeneralized its injected regular-memfd evidence, but its required identity/no-host-call invariant is independently justified by this kernel errseq counterexample. Current /proc root backing has since been corrected to real procfs and is reidentified after SCM_RIGHTS; the remaining dependency is regular synthetic proc and fdinfo carriers. Rejecting outbound regular-proc SCM rights is fail-closed but is itself a native compatibility loss, so it is not the final parity design. A fresh registered write destination now exists at worktrees/slots/kvm-syncfs-identity-20260920, branch codex/kvm-syncfs-identity-20260920, start b13ad926a34f27bb39a349429a1d08e812d741b4. Its first bounded implementation is only the independent prerequisite: seal synthetic proc snapshots and expose an O_RDONLY OFD, repairing the current writable backing; syncfs and identity marking remain out of that initial edit pending self-carrier review.
